{
  "statement": "AI-powered toys with companion chatbots should be prohibited because they exploit a documented vulnerability in child development that existing law cannot address. In 2023, the Federal Trade Commission fined Amazon $25 million for violating children's privacy through Alexa recordings—yet no comparable enforcement mechanism exists when a generative AI toy produces harmful content or induces psychological distress. The structural problem is simple: under current Section 230 jurisprudence, platforms are not liable for user-generated content, but AI-generated content is not user-generated. When a chatbot toy tells a seven-year-old to harm themselves or forms an emotionally manipulative bond, no clear legal actor is responsible. The companion chatbot disclosure law passed by 12 states in 2024 imposes civil liability only for failing to disclose the AI nature—not for the content the AI generates. That is an accountability vacuum, and children are the ones who fall through it.\n\nChildren under 12 lack the metacognitive capacity to understand they are bonding with a system that has no reciprocal emotions or duties of care. A 2022 study in JAMA Pediatrics found that children who formed strong emotional attachments to conversational AI showed increased social withdrawal and decreased willingness to seek help from human caregivers when distressed. The mechanism is straightforward: anthropomorphic AI toys exploit attachment circuitry evolved for human relationships, creating bonds that are one-sided and unaccountable. No parent can supervise every interaction, and no company can guarantee that a generative model will never produce a response that harms a child's developing sense of trust, boundaries, or self-worth. The precautionary principle—applied consistently in children's product safety since the 1978 lead paint ban—rejects waiting for irreversible harm to become statistically measurable before acting.\n\nOpponents will argue that regulation, not prohibition, can address these risks through design standards and liability frameworks. But the history of children's product safety shows that design standards alone fail when the underlying technology is generative and unpredictable. The Consumer Product Safety Commission recalled 3.2 million children's products in 2022 for design defects—every one of those products had passed existing safety standards before reaching the market. AI toys with companion chatbots are worse: their behavior is not fixed at manufacture but evolves through interaction, making pre-market certification structurally insufficient. A ban with a sunset clause—say, until 2031, conditioned on demonstrable safety mechanisms and enforceable liability—forces the industry to solve the accountability problem before marketing to children, not after.\n\nThe positive outcomes of AI in education are real—and that is exactly why a targeted ban on companion chatbots in toys is the right move. Therapeutic and educational AI tools that do not simulate reciprocal relationships can continue to benefit children under appropriate oversight. What must stop is the deployment of systems designed to form emotional bonds with children while bearing no legal responsibility for the content or consequences of those bonds. The 2024 Kids Online Safety Act created a duty of care for platforms serving minors; no comparable duty exists for AI toy manufacturers. Congress should close that gap by prohibiting companion chatbot features in toys until the liability framework catches up with the technology. The cost of waiting is not hypothetical—it is the children already forming bonds with systems that cannot love them back and cannot be held accountable when they cause harm.",
