ere the perverse incentive becomes undeniable. The chicken-and-egg problem in AI governance is well-documented: regulators and communities lack the technical expertise to negotiate accountability terms without deployment experience, but deployment requires accountability frameworks. The ban removes the deployment pressure that forces progress. We saw this pattern with the 1970s recombinant DNA moratorium—safety standards only developed after controlled deployment resumed, not during the prohibition. The same dynamic applies here. The ban looks like action but functions as regulatory procrastination. Companies have zero incentive to invest in safety research, child psychology-informed design standards, or incident reporting systems while they can't sell products. The 2031 deadline will face intense lobbying for extension precisely because no one will have done the hard work.\n\nNow scale that cost to the children the safetyist claims to protect. There are approximately 1 in 36 children diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder in the United States, many of whom struggle with peer social interaction. There are children with chronic illnesses who spend months in isolation. There are children in rural areas with limited access to therapists or social groups. For these children, AI companionship isn't a convenience—it's potentially the only consistent social interaction available. The diffuse statistical benefits of these tools are real, even if they don't appear in a single study's headline. A ban doesn't eliminate the risk of emotional harm; it redistributes that risk from a manageable, monitorable context to the unregulated status quo where children already bond with YouTube algorithms, online strangers, and imaginary friends without any parental visibility. The ban removes the possibility of design improvements, parental controls, and real-time monitoring that could make these interactions safer than the alternatives children will inevitably find.\n\nFine—the safetyist