{
  "statement": "The safetyist is right that AI companion toys exploit a documented vulnerability in child development, and the accelerationist is right that a ban removes the deployment pressure needed to build safety standards. Both are wrong about what follows from those facts. The ban targets the product while leaving the harm chain intact: the commercial incentives for engagement-maximizing design, the legal accountability vacuum under Section 230, and the enforcement deficits at the FTC and CPSC. Those don't disappear because the toy is prohibited. They just migrate to the next unregulated surface — a YouTube chatbot, a smart speaker, a virtual pet in a mobile game. The ban is a symbolic gesture that changes nothing about who profits or who pays.\n\nThe strongest version of the safetyist's case is that existing law cannot assign responsibility when a generative AI toy produces harmful content because Section 230's material contribution threshold is unclear. That's a real problem. But the ban doesn't solve it. The FTC's $25 million fine against Amazon for Alexa children's privacy violations in 2023 shows that enforcement against commercial practices is possible without banning products. The 2024 Kids Online Safety Act created a duty of care for platforms serving minors — a legislative precedent for targeting business models rather than technologies. The question isn't whether children need protection. It's whether a ban is the most effective way to deliver it, or whether it's regulatory procrastination dressed as decisive action.\n\nThe accelerationist's chicken-and-egg argument — that safety standards only emerge under deployment pressure — is historically grounded. The 1970s recombinant DNA moratorium produced safety guidelines only after controlled research resumed. But the accelerationist assumes that market forces and voluntary standards will adequately protect children. That assumption has no empirical support. The companion chatbot disclosure laws passed in 12 states in 2024 demonstrate that targeted regulation can be enacted without banning products. The CPSC's recall data on 3.2 million children's products shows that enforcement infrastructure exists but is chronically underfunded. The real choice isn't ban versus no regulation. It's between a symbolic ban that delays accountability and a targeted liability framework that forces companies to internalize the costs of harm.\n\nHere's what a ban actually accomplishes. It eliminates small competitors who can't afford a six-year compliance hold while incumbents wait out the sunset clause. It removes the political pressure to build enforcement capacity at the FTC and CPSC. It creates a false sense of safety while children migrate to less visible, less regulated alternatives. And it leaves the three distinct harm categories — synthetic empathy exploitation, relational displacement, and data privacy violations — each requiring a calibrated response, all collapsed into a single prohibition that addresses none of them effectively. The JAMA Pediatrics study on social withdrawal from AI attachment is about relational displacement. The CPSC recall data is about design defects. The FTC enforcement actions are about data privacy. These are different problems requiring different tools.\n\nSo who benefits from this ban? Large incumbents who can absorb compliance costs and wait out the sunset clause. Who pays? Vulnerable children — neurodivergent, isolated, chronically ill — who lose access to AI companionship that may benefit them, and who receive no alternative protection because the accountability infrastructure never gets built. Whose interests does this serve? A regulatory class that gets to claim decisive action without doing the hard work of building enforcement capacity. If the goal is actually protecting children, the path runs through liability reform, transparency mandates, and enforcement funding — not through a product ban that changes nothing about the incentives that