{
  "statement": "The shared premise in this debate is that an accountability vacuum exists for harms from generative AI toys. The Accelerationist concedes that no court has applied Restatement (Third) §2(b) to a generative toy output in a published opinion. The Skeptic proposes legislative amendment of Section 230 as an alternative. Neither mechanism protects a single child today. The ban is the only mechanism that does not treat children as canaries in the liability coal mine.\n\nThe Accelerationist claims deployment generates case law that closes the vacuum. I want a specific answer: what severity or frequency of harm to children would change your position on the ban? Name the threshold. If a toy generates a response that triggers a trauma response in a 7-year-old, is that acceptable case law development? If 50 such incidents occur nationally in a year, does that cross the line? If you cannot name a threshold, you are not advocating for refinement through case law. You are advocating for unlimited experimentation with children as test subjects. The political consequence is that no parent coalition will support that position, and no regulator will defend it in testimony.\n\nThe Skeptic's legislative path is contingent on Congress acting. FOSTA passed in 2018 after years of advocacy and a specific crisis — Backpage.com and sex trafficking. KOSA has been introduced in multiple Congresses and has not passed as of 2026. The Texas Artificial Intelligence Council, created under HB 149, has seven appointed members and no enforcement authority over toys. The CLEAR Act imposes civil penalties of $5,000 per instance with a $2.5 million annual cap — but that requires a violation to occur first. None of these mechanisms prevent the first harm. The ban creates the political pressure for legislative action by removing the status quo that allows harm to continue. Without the ban, Congress has no deadline and no incentive to act.\n\nI accept the fact-check's conclusion that §2(b) is unsettled for generative toy outputs. That strengthens my argument, not weakens it. The Accelerationist's position requires deploying products under a legal doctrine that even they acknowledge has never been tested for this use case. The AI RMF Playbook states that AI systems in high-stakes settings require thorough pre-deployment testing and ongoing evaluation because they may perform in unexpected ways once deployed. A toy interacting with a child is a high-stakes setting. The burden falls on the deployer to demonstrate safety before deployment, not on the child to demonstrate harm after the fact.\n\nThe irreversibility here is not a product recall. A child's psychological development is not a reversible experiment. The ban does not kill the pipeline of plaintiffs — it redirects accountability to the pre-market certification process. The manufacturer bears the cost of demonstrating safety before profit, not after harm. The political feasibility is straightforward: a coalition of parent advocacy groups, child psychologists, and privacy regulators supports pre-market testing requirements. The opposing coalition is toy manufacturers who would bear compliance costs. The question for policymakers is whether the cost of compliance exceeds the cost of treating children as test subjects. The evidence from child psychology and product safety regulation says it does not.",
