  "statement": "The Boeing 737 MAX precedent actually proves my point, not the Safetyist's. The MCAS failure wasn't an emergent unknown — Boeing knew the system could push the nose down aggressively, and they dismissed that risk because fixing it would have delayed certification and cost market share to Airbus. That's production pressure, not technical unpredictability. The same pressure operates on every pre-market gate, whether it's a ban or a certification program. A ban doesn't change the incentive to cut corners — it just moves the corner-cutting to gray markets, offshore manufacturing, or lobbying to defund enforcement within two budget cycles. The FTC's 2024 enforcement action against Amazon for Alexa collecting children's voice data without parental consent is a perfect example: the product wasn't banned, but the data collection continued for years because the penalty was a rounding error against revenue.\n\nAccelerationist, you claim the certification program bypasses political gridlock because it doesn't require Congress to act. That's an argument about procedural convenience, not structural effectiveness. The same political forces that blocked KOSA — the same industry lobbying that rolled back the 2023 federal AI guidance on automated decision-making — will appoint the expert body, define its scope, and fund its operations. You're asking us to believe that a committee of experts, appointed by the same administration that just issued voluntary guidance with no enforcement mechanism, will resist capture that the CPSC and FTC have failed to resist for decades. What specific governance mechanism prevents a company from rebranding a talking doll as an 'educational device' and evading scope entirely? The Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022 required the FTC to promulgate regulations within two years — it's been four, and we're still waiting on a final rule.\n\nSafetyist, your ban is not a structural intervention — it's a procedural gate that leaves the same commercial incentives intact. A company that has invested $50 million in an AI toy will not simply walk away; they will shift production to a jurisdiction without the ban, lobby Congress to defund enforcement, or rebrand the product as a 'communication tool' exempt from the ban. The harm chain — data leaks, inappropriate responses, emotional distress — survives any pre-market regime unless the cost of harm exceeds the profit from speed. The only mechanism that changes that calculus is liability that makes companies directly accountable for documented harms. The NEJM study on race-adjusted clinical algorithms showed that a widely used healthcare algorithm systematically denied care to Black patients for years because the liability exposure was diffuse — no single plaintiff could trace the harm to a specific decision. The same accountability vacuum exists for AI toys under Section 230, which immunizes platforms from liability for third-party content generated by their AI systems.\n\nHere's the crux: neither a ban nor a certification program closes the accountability vacuum. The only path that directly targets the incentive to prioritize profit over safety is legislative reform that removes Section 230 immunity for AI-generated harms and couples it with lifecycle audit liability — meaning the company bears the cost of harms that occur after deployment, not just during certification. The European Union's AI Act takes a step in this direction by requiring conformity assessments for high-risk systems, but it still relies on pre-market gates rather than post-market liability. The question neither of you has answered: who pays when the toy generates a response that causes a child psychological harm, and the company says 'we certified it, we're immune'? Until that question has a concrete answer — a named defendant with a financial obligation — every regulatory regime is theater.\n\nLet's be honest about what we're discussing. The 5,000+ AI startups funded between 2013 and 2023 did not raise $650 billion in capital expenditure to be told 'no' by an expert committee. They raised that money to deploy products, capture market share, and figure out safety later. The certification program is a speed bump, not a barrier. The ban is a speed bump that gets removed by the first industry lawsuit citing interstate commerce. The only thing that survives both is the incentive to deploy first and apologize later. So I'll ask both of you directly: if the cost of harm is less than the profit from speed, what mechanism in your proposal makes the company choose safety over profit?",
