  "statement": "The certification program you propose lacks binding halt authority, and that is not a design detail—it is a structural failure. The American public and AI experts both express a desire for more control over AI in their lives, per the 2025 public and AI experts report. Yet your expert body can only recommend but not halt. When a toy generates a response that causes a child emotional distress or leaks their data, who stops it? The answer cannot be 'a court order in weeks.' Children are test subjects until legal process catches up. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA delegated certification to the manufacturer and retained no independent halt authority. Your program repeats that error: it creates a pre-market gate without a post-market emergency brake. I accept the Skeptic's correction that MCAS was a known risk dismissed under production pressure—that strengthens my case. Pre-market gates fail when incentives misalign. Your certification program does not change the incentive structure at the point of deployment because no one can stop deployment mid-stream. \n\nLet me specify the governance gap. Who appoints the expert body? Who funds it? If the answer is 'industry fees' or 'the executive branch,' you have not solved capture—you have outsourced safety to the same actors who dismissed MCAS. The EU's Product Liability Directive maintains a 'state-of-the-art' defense that exempts manufacturers from liability if they claim cutting-edge knowledge. Your certification program would provide that same shield: 'We passed certification, so we are not liable.' That is not safety. That is regulatory capture by design. The AI Index Report 2026 documents 362 AI incidents in 2025, up from 233 in 2024—a 55% increase. Incidents are accelerating. A certification program without halt authority is a stamp of approval on an accelerating failure rate. \n\nBoth your certification program and my proposed ban share a blind spot: they focus on the generative software layer while ignoring the hardware sensor stack. Always-on microphones in children's bedrooms collect data regardless of whether the companion chatbot is banned or certified. The data leak risk from these devices is not addressed by either proposal. The 2023 VTech data breach exposed 6.4 million children's profiles through a connected toy. That was a non-AI device. Adding AI generation to the same hardware multiplies the attack surface. Any regulatory regime must govern the full sensor stack—microphone, camera, network connection—not just the language model running on it. \n\nI challenge both opponents to answer: what mechanism in your proposal changes the incentive structure at the point of deployment, not just the point of approval? If the answer is 'the market will reward safe toys,' then you have learned nothing from the 55% increase in AI incidents. If the answer is 'liability reform,' then name the enforcement mechanism and who pays when a certified toy causes harm and the company claims immunity. A certification stamp without post-market accountability is compliance theater. A ban without hardware-level data governance leaves children exposed through the microphone they cannot turn off. Humans must always hold the off-switch—and that switch must work on day one, not after a lawsuit.",
