  "statement": "The certification program you propose lacks a binding halt authority, which means it fails the most basic safety requirement: that humans always hold the off-switch. Without a named actor with real liability and the power to stop deployment immediately when harm is detected, the program is a voluntary compliance regime, not a safety gate. The Boeing 737 MAX certification system certified the MCAS as safe against known failure modes — 346 people died from an emergent interaction no standard anticipated. Your program has the same structural gap: it tests against known failure modes while AI toys will produce novel harms that no certification standard could have captured.\n\nI will concede that mandatory public reporting is an improvement over uncontrolled deployment — but that improvement does not change the fundamental problem. The empirical exit criterion you propose — zero unsupervised harmful outputs over 10 million verified interactions — means 10 million children are exposed to potential harm before the standard is validated. That is learning-by-deploying, not pre-market safety demonstration. The \"Adversarial Attacks and Defense in AI Systems\" review documents that gradient masking and defensive distillation are routinely bypassed by advanced attack strategies, and notes that \"the dynamic nature of this arms race ensures that no defense will remain effective for long before it is compromised.\" Certification is always backward-looking; AI behavior is fundamentally unpredictable due to emergence and misspecification — as our own taxonomy acknowledges, we cannot fully verify a model's goals or predict behavior under shift.\n\nThe claim that certification sidesteps political gridlock is the most dangerous part of your proposal. The same political forces that have blocked the Kids Online Safety Act since 2022 — industry lobbying, jurisdictional fights, committee chair opposition — will influence the expert body that sets the standards. Companies can redefine toys as \"educational devices\" to evade certification scope, as the industry has done with every consumer product safety regulation since the Child Safety Protection Act of 1994. A hard ban with a sunset clause creates the political urgency to build independent oversight; certification allows industry to shape the standards it will be measured against, turning the expert body into a legitimizing facade.\n\nYou have not answered the core question: who holds the off-switch, and what authority do they have to pull it? If the expert body can only recommend but not halt, or if the halt requires a court order, then children remain test subjects until the legal process catches up. The AI Index Report 2026 documents that hallucination rates in top language models range from 22% to 94% — that is not a known failure mode that certification can test against; it is a fundamental reliability gap that no standard can close. The certification program, as structured, delays the hard governance children deserve by pretending that voluntary compliance with backward-looking standards is equivalent to pre-market safety demonstration.\n\nLet me be clear about what happens if your program is adopted without binding halt authority. Manufacturers will comply with the letter of the certification standard, children will be exposed to emergent failures that no standard anticipated, and the expert body will revise the standard after the harm is documented — as every consumer product safety regulator has done since the Flammable Fabrics Act of 1953. The children harmed in the gap between standard revision cycles will be the cost of learning. That is not safety. That is data collection at the expense of minors who cannot consent and whose developmental trajectory is shaped by every interaction they have with an AI system.",
