hat exempts manufacturers from liability if they pass certification — creating regulatory capture by design, as documented in the source 'Outsmarting Smart Devices: Preparing for AI Liability Risks and Regulations.' A company that passes certification faces no liability for harms that emerge after deployment, removing any incentive to invest in post-market monitoring or recall. The ban avoids this by making liability the default, forcing manufacturers to demand a liability regime before they can sell.\n\nMy sharpest remaining disagreement with the Accelerationist is whether certification can be made capture-resistant without binding halt authority — I say no, and the burden is on them to name a single jurisdiction where a certification program without halt authority has prevented a catastrophic product failure. My disagreement with the Skeptic is whether Section 230 reform can pass Congress given the 99-1 Senate vote — the evidence suggests it cannot, but the ban's sunset clause creates a forcing function that might change that calculation. The ambient surveillance blind spot is a shared failure: all three debaters focused on generative software harms while neglecting the hardware-level data governance for always-on microphones that the 'pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report' found 30% of the public and 24% of AI experts believe will make humans happier — a finding that underscores the disconnect between public optimism and the regulatory vacuum. Any path forward must govern the full sensor stack, not just the language model.",
